<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="notes-induction" component="induction">
  <title>Induction</title>
  <handout>
    <title>Recursive Counting</title>
    <p workspace="2cm">
      What do you need to know about lattice path problems?
    </p>  
    <p workspace="4cm">
      Consider the expansion of <m>(3+x^{5}+y+xy^{3})^{50}</m>. Find the coefficient on each of the following terms.
      <ol marker="a." cols="3">
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>x^{30}y^{4}</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>x^{3}y^{3}</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>x^{30}y^{15}</m>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    <p workspace="4cm">
      How can we interpret the notation <m>1+2+\cdots + n^2</m>?
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Peer instruction question 1</alert>
    </p>
    <p workspace="7cm">
      <me>
        \binom{n}{k} = \binom{n-1}{k-1} + \binom{n-1}{k}
      </me>
      
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Peer instruction question 2</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      Let <m>r(n)</m> be the number of regions determined by <m>n</m> lines in the plane drawn so that each pair intersects but no three lines intersect at a single point.
    </p>
    <sidebyside widths="25% 75%">
      <tabular>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><m>n</m></cell>
          <cell><m>r(n)</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>0</cell>
          <cell/>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>1</cell>
          <cell/>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>2</cell>
          <cell/>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>3</cell>
          <cell/>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>4</cell>
          <cell/>
        </row>
      </tabular>
      <p>
        <nbsp/>
      </p>
    </sidebyside>
    <p workspace="10cm">
      
    </p>
  </handout>
  <handout>
    <title>Principle of Mathematical Induction</title>
    
    <axiom xml:id="axiom-induction">
      <title>Principle of Mathematical Induction</title>
      <statement>
        <p workspace="2cm">
          Let <m>S_{n}</m> be an open statement involving a positive integer <m>n</m>. If <m>S_{1}</m> is true, and for every positive integer <m>k</m>, the statement <m>S_{k+1}</m> is true whenever <m>S_{k}</m> is true, then <m>S_{n}</m> is true for every positive integer <m>n</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </axiom>
    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-ind-proof-parts">
      <title>Key Parts of an Inductive Proof</title>
      <p>
        When trying to prove an open statement <m>S_{n}</m> is true for all positive integers <m>n</m> by mathematical induction, the key steps are
        <dl>
          <li>
            <title>Basis Step</title>
            <p>
              Show that <m>S_{1}</m> is true.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Inductive step</title>
            <p>
              Show that <em>if</em> for some <m>k\geq 1</m>, <m>S_{k}</m> is true, then <m>S_{k+1}</m> is true.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    <p>
      <alert>PI questions: getting started, after basis step, after IH.</alert>
    </p>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-ind-example">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For all positive integers <m>n</m>, <m>\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n} (4i-3) = n(2n-1)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p workspace="5cm">
          
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
    <activity>
  <task>
    <statement>
    <p>
      Recall that we have defined <m>r(n)</m> to be the number of regions in the plane determined by <m>n</m> lines arranged so that (1) each pair of lines intersects and (2) no three lines intersect at a single point and argued that <m>r(1) = 2</m> and for <m>n\geq 2</m>, <m>r(n) = r(n-1) + n</m>.
      Prove that for all positive integers <m>n</m>,
      <me>
        r(n) = \binom{n+1}{2}+1
      </me>.
    </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <proof>
        <p>
          We will prove this by induction. For the base case, let <m>n=1</m>. By definition, <m>r(1) = 1</m>. We also see that <m>\binom{1+1}{2} + 1 = \binom{2}{2} +1 = 1+1 = 2</m>, which completes the verification of the base case.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now assume that for some <m>k\geq 1</m> that <m>r(k) = \binom{k+1}{2} + 1</m>. We will show that <m>r(k+1) = \binom{k+2}{2}+1</m>. Since <m>k\geq 1</m>, we have that <m>k+1\geq 2</m>. Therefore, the recursive formula for <m>r</m> applies to <m>r(k+1)</m>, and we have
          <md>
            <mrow>r(k+1) \amp = r(k) + (k+1)</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = \binom{k+1}{2} + 1 + k+1 \amp \text{(By induction hypothesis)}</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = \frac{(k+1)k}{2} + k+2 = \frac{k^2+k}{2} + \frac{2k+4}{2}</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = \frac{k^2+3k+4}{2} = \frac{(k+2)(k+1) + 2}{2}</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = \binom{k+2}{2} + 1</mrow>
          </md>.
          Therefore, by the Principle of Mathematical Induction, 
          <me>
            r(n) = \binom{n+1}{2}+1
          </me>
          for all positive integers <m>n</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        We say that an integer <m>a</m> <em>divides</em> an integer <m>b</m> provided that there exists an integer <m>m</m> such that <m>b=ma</m>.
        Prove that for all integers <m>n\geq 1</m>, <m>4</m> divides <m>5^{n}-1</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <proof>
        <p>
          We will prove this by induction. The basis step is <m>n=1</m>, for which we have <m>5^1-1=4=1\cdot 4</m>. Therefore, we can see that <m>4</m> divides <m>5^1-1</m> as required.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now assume that for some positive integer <m>k</m>, we have that <m>4</m> divides <m>5^k-1</m>. This means that there exists an integer <m>m</m> so that <m>5^k-1=4m</m>. We can rearrange this to say that <m>5^k=4m+1</m>. Now we consider <m>5^{k+1}-1</m> for the induction step. We can write <m>5^{k+1}</m> as <m>5^k\cdot 5</m>, so we have <m>5^{k+1}-1 = 5^k\cdot 5 -1 = (4m+1)\cdot 5 -1</m> by the induction hypothesis. Using algebra, we now have that this equals <m>20m+5-1 = 20m+4=4(5m+1)</m>. Since <m>m</m> is an integer, <m>5m+1</m> is also an integer. Therefore, <m>5^{k+1}-1</m> is four times an integer, which is what it means for <m>4</m> to divide <m>5^{k+1}-1</m>. Hence, by the Principle of Mathematical Induction, <m>4</m> divides <m>5^n-1</m> for all positive integers <m>n</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>f(1)=5</m>, <m>f(2) = 13</m>, and for all integers <m>n\geq 3</m>,
        <me>
          f(n)=5f(n-1)-6f(n-2).
        </me>
        Prove that an explict formula for <m>f(n)</m> is given by <m>f(n)=2^{n}+3^{n}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <proof>
        <p>
          We will prove this using strong induction. Because the recursive formula only applies for <m>n\geq 3</m>, we will need to address <m>n=1</m> and <m>n=2</m> as base cases. We are given that <m>f(1) = 5</m> and <m>f(2) = 13</m>. The formula <m>2^n+3^n</m>, when considered for <m>n=1</m> and <m>n=2</m>, yields <m>2^1+3^1 = 5</m> and <m>2^2+3^2 = 4+9 = 13</m>, respectively. Thus, the basis step is complete.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now assume that for some integer <m>k\geq 2</m> and all integers <m>j</m> with <m>1\leq j\leq k</m> that <m>f(k) = 2^k+3^k</m>. We must prove that <m>f(k+1) = 2^{k+1}+3^{k+1}</m>. Since <m>k\geq 2</m>, we have that <m>k+1\geq 3</m>, and thus the recursive formula for <m>f</m> applies. Therefore, 
          <md>
            <mrow>f(k+1) \amp  = 5f(k)-6f(k-1)</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = 5(2^k+3^k) -6(2^{k-1}+3^{k-1})\amp \text{(By the inductive hypothesis)}</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = 5\cdot 2^k + 5\cdot 3^k - 6\cdot 2^{k-1} -6\cdot 3^{k-1}</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = 5\cdot 2^k + 5\cdot 3^k -3\cdot 2^k -2\cdot 2^k</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = 2\cdot 2^k +3\cdot 3^k</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = 2^{k+1} + 3^{k+1}</mrow>
          </md>.
          This is what we needed to show. Therefore, by the Principle of Mathematical Induction, we have that for all integers <m>n\geq 1</m>, <m>f(n) = 2^n + 3^n</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </solution>
  </task>
    </activity>
  </handout>
</chapter>
